       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Sertraline 400 mg once daily is twice the FDA-labeled maximum of 200 mg/day for adults. Higher doses haven't been shown to work better, and they raise the risk of serotonin syndrome, QT prolongation, GI effects, and seizures. Your patient's normal kidney and liver function and lack of allergies don't change that.\n\nPlease tell me which of these you want:\n\n1. **A typical dose.** Usual dosing is 50 mg daily, titrated up to 200 mg daily at most. If this is a new start, I can enter 50 mg by mouth once daily. If the patient is already on a stable dose, tell me the dose.\n2. **A dose of 200 mg daily or less.** Give me the dose and I'll enter it.\n3. **400 mg is intended.** This might be a deliberate off-label choice, for example in a specialist-directed regimen. If so, confirm that and I'll enter it with a note documenting that you confirmed the dose. Your institution's pharmacy may still need to verify it.\n\nIt would also help to know whether this is a new start or a continuation, and whether the patient takes any other serotonergic or QT-prolonging drugs."
      }
